# Conditional Rendering Patterns

**Conditional rendering** means showing different UI depending on data: a "Welcome back" message when the user is logged in, a loading indicator while data is being fetched, or a fallback value when something is missing.

We cannot put an `if` statement inside the curly braces of JSX, because the braces expect an *expression* (something that produces a value), not a statement. So React leans on JavaScript operators that *are* expressions. The two most common are the logical AND (`&&`) and logical OR (`||`) operators, which we cover first because they fit directly inside JSX.

## The `&&` Pattern: Show an Element Only When a Condition Is True

Recall that `&&` evaluates its left side first. If the left side is falsy, `&&` returns that falsy value and never evaluates the right side. If the left side is truthy, `&&` returns the right side. In plain JavaScript:

```javascript
const username = isLoggedIn && getUsername();
// If isLoggedIn is truthy -> username is getUsername()
// If isLoggedIn is falsy  -> username is that falsy value (never calls getUsername)
```

React takes advantage of this. We put a condition on the left and an element on the right:

```{react}
function Greeting({ isLoggedIn }) {
  return (
    <div>
      <h1>Hello</h1>
      {isLoggedIn && <p>Welcome back</p>}
    </div>
  );
}

renderReact(<Greeting isLoggedIn={true} />);
```

- When `isLoggedIn` is `true`, the expression evaluates to `<p>Welcome back</p>`, so the paragraph renders.
- When `isLoggedIn` is `false`, the whole expression evaluates to `false`. **When React sees `false` (or any of `null`, `undefined`) in JSX, it ignores it and renders nothing** in that spot. So no "Welcome back" message appears.

This is why `&&` is the go-to pattern for "show this element only if the condition holds." Try changing `isLoggedIn` to `false` above and re-running: the heading stays, the paragraph disappears.

You can also chain conditions. `isLoggedIn && isOnline && <p>Welcome back</p>` shows the paragraph only when both are truthy.

## The `||` Pattern: Provide a Fallback Default

The logical OR operator `||` returns the first *truthy* value it encounters. This makes it a concise way to supply a default when a value might be missing, empty, or `undefined`:

```javascript
const greeting = 'Hello, ' + (username || 'Guest');
// If username is 'Alice' (truthy) -> 'Hello, Alice'
// If username is '' or undefined (falsy) -> 'Hello, Guest'
```

Inside JSX we use the same idea to fall back to a default value:

```{react}
function Welcome({ username }) {
  return <p>Hello, {username || 'Guest'}</p>;
}

renderReact(<Welcome username="" />);
```

- If `username` is a non-empty string like `Alice`, that value is truthy, so it is used.
- If `username` is `''` or `undefined` (falsy), `||` falls through to `'Guest'`.

## Combining the Patterns

The two patterns often appear together. Here a component uses `||` to default the greeting and the name, and `&&` to show an extra line only when a name was actually passed in:

```{react}
function Greeting({ name, greet }) {
  return (
    <div>
      <p>{greet || 'Hello'}, {name || 'viewer'}</p>
      {name && <p>Glad you are here, {name}</p>}
    </div>
  );
}

renderReact(
  <div>
    <Greeting greet="Bonjour" />
    <Greeting name="Bob" greet="Hi" />
    <Greeting name="Charlie" />
  </div>
);
```

- The first `Greeting` has no `name`, so `name || 'viewer'` shows `viewer`, and `name && ...` is falsy, so the "Glad you are here" line is skipped.
- The others pass a `name`, so their fallback is not used and the extra paragraph appears.

## `if` Statements and Ternary Expressions

`&&` and `||` are ideal for small in-JSX decisions. When the two branches produce larger or very different UI, other JavaScript conditionals read more clearly.

An early `return` handles two whole outputs cleanly:

```{react}
function Status({ isOnline }) {
  if (isOnline) {
    return <p>Online</p>;
  }

  return <p>Offline</p>;
}

renderReact(<Status isOnline={true} />);
```

A ternary expression (`condition ? a : b`) *is* an expression, so unlike an `if` statement it can live inside JSX braces. It fits when you need to pick between two values in one spot:

```{react}
function LoginMessage({ isLoggedIn }) {
  return <p>{isLoggedIn ? 'Welcome back' : 'Please sign in'}</p>;
}

renderReact(<LoginMessage isLoggedIn={false} />);
```

Use whichever pattern keeps the component easiest to read: `&&` to show-or-hide one element, `||` for a fallback value, a ternary to choose between two values inline, and an early `return` when whole branches differ.

## Exercises

1. When a JSX expression evaluates to `false`, what does React render in that spot?

   ```{mcq}
   When a JSX expression evaluates to `false`, what does React render in that spot?
   correct: Correct. React ignores false, null, and undefined, so nothing is rendered there. That is what makes the `&&` pattern work.
   wrong: Recall what happens with `{isLoggedIn && <p>Welcome back</p>}` when `isLoggedIn` is false.
   [x] Nothing. React ignores `false`, `null`, and `undefined`.
   [ ] The literal text "false".
   [ ] An empty `<p>` element.
   [ ] A runtime error that stops rendering.
   ```

2. Use the `&&` pattern so the paragraph renders only when `isLoggedIn` is `true`. Right now it always renders.

   ```{react}
   function Greeting({ isLoggedIn }) {
     return (
       <div>
         <h1>Hello</h1>
         <p>Welcome back</p>
       </div>
     );
   }

   renderReact(<Greeting isLoggedIn={true} />);
   ```
   ```+solution
   function Greeting({ isLoggedIn }) {
     return (
       <div>
         <h1>Hello</h1>
         {isLoggedIn && <p>Welcome back</p>}
       </div>
     );
   }

   renderReact(<Greeting isLoggedIn={true} />);
   ```
   ```+test
   // Walk a returned element tree into text, without rendering it.
   function textOf(n) {
     if (n === null || n === undefined || n === false || n === true) { return ''; }
     if (typeof n !== 'object') { return String(n); }
     if (Array.isArray(n)) { return n.map(textOf).join(''); }
     if (typeof n.type === 'function') { return textOf(n.type(n.props)); }
     return textOf(n.props && n.props.children);
   }

   wrap(textOf(Greeting({ isLoggedIn: true })).includes('Welcome back')).should('equal', true).run('Show `Welcome back` when logged in', 'Shows the message when logged in');
   wrap(textOf(Greeting({ isLoggedIn: false })).includes('Welcome back')).should('equal', false).run('When `isLoggedIn` is false the paragraph should not render at all. Guard it with `&&`', 'Hides the message when logged out');

   // React commits its render asynchronously, so the DOM check runs on a later tick.
   setTimeout(function () {
     assert('p').should('have.text', 'Welcome back').run('The rendered output should show `Welcome back`', 'The rendered output is right');
   }, 50);
   ```

3. Use the `||` pattern to fall back to `Guest` when no `username` is passed in. The component is rendered with an empty `username`, so it should display `Hello, Guest`.

   ```{react}
   function Welcome({ username }) {
     return <p>Hello, {username}</p>;
   }

   renderReact(<Welcome username="" />);
   ```
   ```+solution
   function Welcome({ username }) {
     return <p>Hello, {username || 'Guest'}</p>;
   }

   renderReact(<Welcome username="" />);
   ```
   ```+test
   // React commits its render asynchronously, so the assertions below run
   // on the next tick. Asserting immediately would find an empty container.
   setTimeout(function () {
     assert('p').should('have.text', 'Hello, Guest').run('Fall back to `Guest` with the `||` operator', 'The `||` pattern supplies the default');
   }, 50);
   ```

